Appendix F Statistical Test Results
We conduct paired t t -tests on Fisher z z -transformed Spearman correlations for each medium-sized model and concept pair in the argument-generation dataset. Because Spearman correlations are bounded and skewed near 1, we first apply the Fisher transformation. For each sample, we compute the per-sample difference between the single- and dual-concept Fisher values and perform a one-sided paired t t -test.
The null hypothesis is that the mean difference is zero, corresponding to no change in performance, while the alternative hypothesis is that single-concept control is stronger than dual-concept control. The resulting t t -statistics and one-sided p p -values are reported below. Large positive t t -values accompanied by extremely small p p -values indicate that introducing a secondary concept significantly harms controllability.
